<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">South Korea</h1><p class="st-meta">1948 CE – present · capital Seoul</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Republic on the southern half of the Korean peninsula, founded in 1948, which moved from dictatorships to democracy in 1987 and grew into a major economy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s surrender in 1945 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> was divided, and negotiations on reunification failed. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-states-army-military-government-in-korea">United States-occupied southern zone</a> became the Republic of Korea in August 1948, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syngman-rhee">Syngman Rhee</a>, a former head of the Korean Provisional Government, winning the first presidential election. The UN declared his government the only lawful government in Korea based on observed elections.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a> invaded on 25 June 1950, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> ended in 1953 with an armistice that South Korea never signed; approximately 3 million people died. A student uprising forced Rhee out in 1960, and General Park Chung Hee took power in a coup in 1961, overseeing export-led growth with political repression until his assassination in 1979. Chun Doo-hwan then seized power, and his forces suppressed the Gwangju uprising of May 1980, killing probably 500 to 2,000 civilians. The June Democratic Struggle of 1987 ended the period of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> and led to the Sixth Republic.</p><h2>To the present</h2><p>Kim Dae-jung, a former political prisoner, became president in 1998 and received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 2000. Park Geun-hye was removed from office in 2017 after protests over a corruption scandal. President Yoon Suk Yeol declared martial law on 3 December 2024, the National Assembly voted it down, and he was impeached on 14 December 2024 and removed on 4 April 2025. Lee Jae-myung took office on 4 June 2025.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/south-korea.jpg" alt="South Korea" width="330" height="220"><figcaption>Image: Original: Government of the Republic of Korea Vector: Great Brightstar and others · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_South_Korea.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1948 CE – present</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Seoul</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Syngman Rhee, Park Chung Hee, Chun Doo-hwan, Kim Dae-jung, Lee Jae-myung</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity (Protestant and Roman Catholic), Buddhism; about half of the population declares no affiliation</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Korean</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of Korea</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1987&amp;focus=south-korea" aria-label="South Korea on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7853 826 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where South Korea lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8173 1082L8173 1082"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8173 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8173 1082h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8173 1082h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Seoul. <a href="/atlas?year=1987&amp;focus=south-korea">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
